# android.hardware.gatekeeper@1.0-service with gatekeeper.mt6797.so
# (= libMcGatekeeper.so, the Trustonic/MTK normal-world half of the TEE
# gatekeeper).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# FACT (`readelf`/`strings` on /vendor/lib64/hw/gatekeeper.mt6797.so): the blob
# is built from
#   vendor/mediatek/proprietary/trustzone/trustonic/source/external/gatekeeper/
#       common/302d/src/GateKeeperAdaptationLayer.cpp
# It links the platform's libgatekeeper.so (imports gatekeeper::GateKeeper::
# Verify/Enroll/DoVerify/ComputeRetryTimeout) and implements the two hooks that
# library leaves to the vendor, GetFailureRecord() and WriteFailureRecord(), on
# top of plain stdio — fopen/fseeko/ftello/__fread_chk/__fwrite_chk/unlink —
# against a path relative to the process working directory. Its error strings
# ("Can't create failure record file.", "Problem occured when writing failure
# record to a file.") are printed with printf(), not liblog.
#
# FACT (AOSP system/gatekeeper/gatekeeper.cpp): Verify() treats a handle whose
# version >= HANDLE_VERSION_THROTTLE (2) as throttled and calls
# GetFailureRecord() *before* it compares the password; if that call fails it
# returns ERROR_UNKNOWN. Handles this device produces are version 2.
#
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-16, direct binder probe of gatekeeperd with no
# framework in the loop, scratch uid 200000): with the HAL's working directory
# left at init's "/" (a read-only rootfs), enroll returns rc=0 and a valid
# hardware-backed handle while verify returns rc=-1 for the correct password
# and rc=-1 for a wrong one. With the working directory changed to
# /data/misc/gatekeeper — same binary, same blob — verify returns rc=0 for the
# correct password and rc=-1 for a wrong one, and the module creates its record
# file there (000000000000000200000.rec). That is the whole defect: a PIN could
# be set but never verified, so no fingerprint could be enrolled.
#
# tools/patches/gatekeeper-hal-service-chdir.patch makes the passthrough
# service chdir("/data/misc/gatekeeper") before it loads the module, restoring
# the pre-Treble contract in which gatekeeperd hosted the module and chdir()ed
# to exactly that directory (init starts it as
# "gatekeeperd /data/misc/gatekeeper"). This file grants the HAL the access
# that move needs. Neither half works alone.
#
# SCOPE OF THE RULES
#
# The directory is created by system/core/rootdir/init.rc:431 as
#   mkdir /data/misc/gatekeeper 0700 system system
# and the HAL runs as user system (its own .rc: user system, group system), so
# DAC already fits; only the label was missing. gatekeeperd holds the identical
# pair in system/sepolicy/public/gatekeeperd.te:33-34 — this is the same data,
# now written by the process that actually owns the module.
#
# Traversal of /data and /data/misc needs nothing extra:
# system/sepolicy/public/domain.te:222 already carries
#   allow domain system_data_file:dir search;
# unconditionally (not inside a treble guard).
#
# There is no neverallow against gatekeeper_data_file by name anywhere in
# system/sepolicy (checked by grep). But gatekeeper_data_file IS a
# core_data_file_type (public/file.te:256), and full_treble_only() policy keeps
# core_data_file_type away from a non-coredomain HAL (public/domain.te:830).
#
# CORRECTION 2026-09-09 (enforcing lane). The old note said those blocks were
# inert because the device is PRODUCT_FULL_TREBLE_OVERRIDE := false
# "(lineage_m95.mk:26)". No such assignment exists -- line 26 there is a VoLTE
# comment -- and BoardConfig.mk:113 sets it to TRUE, so the blocks are live.
# The two rules below were in fact dropped by the R-split port for exactly that
# reason, and are back now on the sanctioned footing: violators.te gives
# hal_gatekeeper_default the data_between_core_and_vendor_violators attribute,
# which is the exemption AOSP provides for this case.
#
# The advice in the last sentence still stands and is now the tightening step,
# not the hypothetical: moving the module's working directory to
# /data/vendor/gatekeeper with its own vendor type would let the exemption go.
#
# The file names in this directory do not collide: gatekeeperd writes the
# per-uid secure-user-id files named in decimal ("0", "100000", 8 bytes each),
# the module writes "<zero-padded uid>.rec".
allow hal_gatekeeper_default gatekeeper_data_file:dir rw_dir_perms;
allow hal_gatekeeper_default gatekeeper_data_file:file create_file_perms;
